The `setInterval` callback is `async`, but `setInterval` discards the returned Promise. If `server.waitForRequestsIdle()` rejects (e.g. because the server was closed between the size check and the await), the rejection becomes an unhandled Promise rejection that can crash the Node.js process. Wrap the body in a `try/catch` and silently return on failure:
```ts
const waitForModuleGraph = setInterval(() => {
  if (server.moduleGraph.fileToModulesMap.size > 0) {
    clearInterval(waitForModuleGraph);
    server.waitForRequestsIdle().then(() => watcherChangeHandler?.()).catch(() => {});
  }
}, 1000);
```

If one listener throws synchronously, the exception propagates out of `forEach`, and all subsequent listeners in the set are never called. Wrap each invocation in a per-listener try/catch so a misbehaving subscriber cannot silently suppress notifications for others:
```ts
listeners.forEach((listener) => {
  try { listener(moduleGraph); } catch {}
});
```
